-- Two exported decisions read an input called `rate`. One writes TYPICALLY 3 and
-- the other writes nothing, and OpenFisca gives an input with no TYPICALLY its
-- own default for the type (0.0). One variable, one default: taking 3 would
-- change what the second decision answers, so the export refuses and says so.
-- (TYPICALLY 0 against none is NOT a disagreement: the variable is the same.)

@export Scaled amount
GIVEN base IS A NUMBER
      rate IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 3
GIVETH A NUMBER
scaled base rate MEANS base * rate

@export Capped amount
GIVEN base IS A NUMBER
      rate IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
capped base rate MEANS (base * rate) - 1
